{
	title: 'Plugins register useEffect style: the body sets up, its return cleans up',
	spec: "src/host/plugins.ts exports the Plugin type and constructs each owner-scoped API as an explicit object: const plugin: Plugin = { before(...) { ... }, after(...) { ... }, around(...) { ... }, set(...) { ... } }; return plugin. Each public method has a visible named implementation; shared work may delegate to named helpers. The interface contains only before, after and around for functions, and set for data. Data remains data rather than being wrapped in functions for customization. A plugin exports a registration function whose body performs setup and registers hooks or data overrides, then returns its cleanup function. Hooks and overrides take effect when registered, so ordinary setup code, including a redraw, can run before or after registration and observe the corresponding behavior. The loader does not redraw automatically. Removing a version removes its hooks and data overrides before running its returned cleanup; removing an override exposes the remaining override or original value under the existing composition rules. plugin.unload and plugin.onChange do not exist. Preserve existing hook composition, ownership, validation and complete error reporting. Do not add automatic migrations or compatibility APIs. Update the tracked example plugin with the API change. Focus behavioral tests on immediate registration, returned cleanup, and restoration of hooks and data. The registration function is a normal synchronous function; its return value is the cleanup function or nothing, never a Promise. It may start asynchronous work (such as a redraw), but registration is final when the body returns. Setup must not throw: a plugin that fails to import or throws in its body is like a config file that does not parse. Hal adds no rollback, staging or last-working-version recovery. Its one recovery step is renaming the file to <name>.ts.broken (the loader loads only .ts files), so the next start does not load it again; then the complete error and both paths reach the user, and Hal keeps running without that plugin. Fix the file and rename it back to enable it. Replacing a version removes the old version's hooks and overrides, runs its cleanup, then runs the new body.",
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		'Coordinate manual conversion of local plugins with the API change; no separate task, automatic migration or compatibility code. Local plugin edits stay outside Git. Update the tracked example plugin in Git with the implementation.',
		'Registration is synchronous and final when the body returns: hooks register straight into the live chains (no staging recorder, generation checks only around the import), and a later call to the API throws. A failed import or body deactivates the old version too, then renames the file to .ts.broken; the entry keeps the error for /plugins. The rename is skipped when the file changed since it was read (still being written; the next load follows). Themes repaint with terminal.redraw() in the body and return () => terminal.redraw(). Tests that watch a directory must not keep non-plugin modules in it, or they get renamed .broken.'
	],
	needs: ['an'],
	foldInto: ['an']
